#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522823} :activity/id #uuid "253331d9-b77f-57ef-b00e-dd4a38f9cd0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/id #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" :topic/math-academy-id 1401} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc41bcd-3417-5400-a54c-1906072f0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522090} {:answer/id #uuid "280e7321-16c9-5e68-b059-fc36a133c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522111} {:answer/id #uuid "809d9373-5abe-50a9-9050-66157bd65485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522276} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ba871-8164-5731-8210-1d7c5fc1f375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522298} {:answer/id #uuid "c69dbee9-0364-537c-b998-1950da1608c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522111} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d7ddac-9aa2-5f5d-a70f-3d898b46caf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7127aac-4823-5d27-b379-06474be0fe41" :question/math-academy-id "q-126913" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "1c438b31-61c8-58dc-acc5-f28ab811272c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a45c74-9b1e-5441-8919-df0ab8e1e247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522021} {:answer/id #uuid "08312884-c6ea-53f3-93c8-ff127f67b413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522031} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5a8fb6-b599-5596-9b2f-c97544575c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522047} {:answer/id #uuid "426a15c7-aeeb-5693-ad27-b576c0204873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522163} {:answer/id #uuid "70327205-4e06-5633-84e8-ec7b8626d9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522047} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c47e5c7-7541-5b18-a52f-c2f68957f291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1ac1f29-7051-5497-b0bb-1e7a6a92e87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149686" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "7b36ab65-7aff-5752-af77-f619ccede402" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d8306d-4b33-5cb2-8dc6-cfdc042ca82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522159} {:answer/id #uuid "4d614b79-144b-5ba2-a516-545fad2f6222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522186} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0cf450-3b58-5d82-b9a3-ea1fd674b459" :answer/type